const body = `<h1>Inheritance (Season 2): Quick Reference</h1>

<p>One page, no story, no lab. The shapes, signatures, and traps of the unit,
compressed. This is the page to keep open during builds and to re-read the
night before a test. Print it and mark it up.</p>

<h2>extends</h2>
<pre><code>public class GameCharacter {          // parent
    protected String name;            // protected: kids can touch
    public GameCharacter(String name) { this.name = name; }
    public String attack() { return name + " swings"; }
}

public class Dragon extends GameCharacter {
    public Dragon(String name) { super(name); }      // parent constructor FIRST
    @Override
    public String attack() { return name + " breathes fire"; }
}</code></pre>

<h2>The Vocabulary</h2>
<table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Means</th></tr>
    <tr><td>superclass / subclass</td><td>parent / child class</td></tr>
    <tr><td>super(...)</td><td>call the parent constructor; must be the first line</td></tr>
    <tr><td>super.method()</td><td>call the parent's version of an overridden method</td></tr>
    <tr><td>@Override</td><td>annotation: compiler checks you really overrode</td></tr>
    <tr><td>abstract class</td><td>cannot be instantiated; may declare abstract methods</td></tr>
    <tr><td>polymorphism</td><td>one variable type, many runtime behaviors</td></tr>
    <tr><td>downcast</td><td>(Dragon) c after instanceof check</td></tr>
</table>


<h2>Polymorphism in One Snippet</h2>
<pre><code>ArrayList&lt;GameCharacter&gt; party = new ArrayList&lt;GameCharacter&gt;();
party.add(new Dragon("Smaug"));
party.add(new Goblin("Grish"));
for (GameCharacter c : party)
    System.out.println(c.attack());   // each calls ITS OWN attack</code></pre>


<h2>The Unit's Traps, One Line Each</h2>
<ul>
    <li>super(...) must be the constructor's first line, or the compiler inserts super() and fails if the parent lacks a no-arg constructor.</li>
    <li>Overriding requires the SAME signature. Different parameters is overloading, a different method.</li>
    <li>Protected is visible to subclasses AND the package. Private is invisible to children.</li>
    <li>Static methods do not override: they hide. Polymorphism needs instance methods.</li>
</ul>

<h2>See Also</h2>
